# FastAPI coding style

Narrows `python`'s coding style to FastAPI conventions; everything not FastAPI-specific comes from `python`'s `rules/coding-style.mdc`.

## Path operations

- Name path operation functions for the action (`read_item`, `create_user`), not the HTTP method.
- Group routes behind an `APIRouter` per resource (`router = APIRouter(prefix="/items", tags=["items"])`) once the project has more than a handful of endpoints.
- Declare an explicit `response_model` (or a return type annotation FastAPI can use as one) on every path operation that returns a Pydantic model or ORM object.
- Declare parameters with `Annotated[<type>, Depends(...)]`/`Annotated[<type>, Query(...)]`, not the bare `= Depends(...)` default form, unless the project consistently uses the older form.

## Pydantic models

- Define request/response schemas as Pydantic `BaseModel` subclasses, not bare `dict`/`Any` parameters (see `rules/patterns.mdc`).
- Keep field constraints on the field (`Field(gt=0, le=100)`, `Literal[...]`), not re-validated in the path operation body.
- Use separate input and output models when they differ by even one field.
- Configure with `model_config = ConfigDict(...)` (Pydantic v2), not the v1 `class Config:`; confirm the version in `pyproject.toml` first.

## Dependencies

- Keep a dependency small and single-purpose; split one that both authenticates and performs business logic.
- Prefer a `yield`-based dependency for anything needing teardown over a plain-return dependency plus a manual close.

## Project layout

- Match the project's existing layout (`src/<package>/` with `routers/`, `models/`/`schemas/`, `dependencies.py`, `main.py`, or a single `main.py` for a small service); do not introduce a second structure.
- Keep `app = FastAPI(...)` and top-level middleware/router registration in one place (typically `main.py`).
